    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start. For the lesson though, I want to make sure the students understand why this is so much more powerful than just 'procedural due process.' Could you explain why the procedural side is often seen as insufficient for actually protecting people's rights, whereas substantive due process is the real driver of progress?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Procedural due process only guarantees fair *methods* when the government deprives someone of life, liberty, or property, such as a proper hearing. However, it doesn't stop the government from creating fundamentally unfair or oppressive laws in the first place. Substantive due process is the powerful antidote because it questions the *content* of the law itself, asking whether the right being restricted is a fundamental liberty worth protecting. Therefore, it is the core mechanism that drives legal progress beyond just following established rules.",
      "source": "target",
